Abstract
Modern coding agents are fundamentally reshaping the role of human input for computational research: they write code, launch in silico experiments, analyze their results, and autonomously operate with ever-increasing time horizons.
Despite these developments, fully autonomous research agents suffer from a limited ‘ideation radius’ and myopic hill-climbing search dynamics.
To bridge this gap, we introduce The Agentic Scientist, an end-to-end distributed pipeline for human researchers to conduct computational research alongside modern coding agents.
Building on its predecessor (Lu et al., 2026, The AI Scientist,), our system decomposes research into task drafting, idea generation, planning, sandboxed experimentation, result aggregation, baseline and ablation construction, post-hoc analysis, and report writing.
Importantly, each step in the pipeline is powered by the next generation of headless coding agents and leverages agentic self-verification.
The overall research process is driven by a meta-controller that manages parallel research agents and enforces rubric verification at each stage.
This design enables the same orchestration layer to be reused across heterogeneous domains with different execution environments, agentic coding harnesses, and evaluation metrics. Our system can be used across a spectrum of autonomy: either as a co-scientist tool, or to perform end-to-end scientific investigation.
Within the co-scientist setting, we leveraged a development version of our system to submit a single research paper, which got accepted for publication at ALIFE 2026 (Yamada et al., 2026).
We further apply The Agentic Scientist fully autonomously to open problem-solving tasks in machine learning, and demonstrate the system’s capability of scientific discovery across different domains of computational research, including: a hypernetwork-based inductive bias for rapid Transformer adaptation and a meta-trained evolution strategy that matches or exceeds CMA-ES on 5 of 7 unseen Brax control tasks.
Our ablations suggest that reliable scientific autonomy depends not only on base model capabilities, but also on the cross-agent orchestration layer.
We open-source The Agentic Scientist and its figure generation tool, autoillustrate, to aid collective development of automated (co-)scientist systems:
Agentic Scientist
https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist-v3
autoillustrate
https://github.com/SakanaAI/Autoillustrate
Figure 1: The Agentic Scientist Four Stage Workflow.
1 Introduction
Large Language Model (LLM)-based tool-calling agents can now complete increasingly long coding tasks with minimal human intervention (Kwa et al., 2025).
Computational research, however, is arguably more than a single long coding task: a practical system supporting human scientists must generate hypotheses, execute controlled experiments, validate claims, iterate on ideas, and communicate the results.
Existing AI scientist systems demonstrate parts of this loop (Lu et al., 2024b; Yamada et al., 2025; Schmidgall et al., 2025; Tang et al., 2025; Jansen et al., 2025; Karpathy, 2026), but myopic hill-climbing can make agents behave merely as flexible hyperparameter optimization engines (Ferreira et al., 2026; Van Stein et al., 2024).
Here, we tackle the systems-level gap between an agent that writes code and a system that can conduct reliable research.
We introduce The Agentic Scientist (Figure 1), a human-steerable, agentic scaffold for end-to-end computational research.
The workflow drafts a task specification from a text description, coordinates parallel sandboxed workers, accumulates retrospective evidence, and writes a manuscript grounded in the results.
During the investigation phase, parallel workers revise scientific hypotheses in response to implementation failures, measured outcomes, and shared performance notes.
The resulting artifacts seed subsequent experiments and remain available for retrospective analysis, ablation design, and manuscript writing.
Predecessors of our system (Table 1) relied on initial implementation directions and loose assessment of the idea implementation. This could result in weak experiment validation and overfitting. The Agentic Scientist, on the other hand, uses self-drafted rubrics-based verification and CLI agents for broader code edits and information protocols.
