A development version of our system was used to automate parts of the research process on Error Diffusion (Fujita, 2026; Kaneko, 2000). Afterwards, we submitted a single paper which was accepted as an ALIFE 2026 talk (Yamada et al., 2026).
We further demonstrate our framework across three Machine Learning research directions: our system discovers composition and optimization biases for hypernetwork-based LLM adaptation (Charakorn et al., 2026).
The Agentic Scientist also extends research on neural network-parametrized meta-black-box optimization (Ma et al., 2025), yielding an optimizer now integrated in evosax (Lange, 2023).
Finally, our system manages to iteratively improve an RL-based LLM fine-tuning program by combining recent algorithmic advances.
All of these demonstrate the broad application of the system to enhance scientific knowledge beyond mere hill-climbing applications.
Our main contributions are:
1.
The Agentic Scientist, a (co-)scientist orchestration scaffold for human-steerable ideation, parallel execution, shared memory, rubric-based verification, and paper manuscript writing.
2.
Co-scientist paper conference acceptance. A paper created with assistance by The Agentic Scientist on backpropagation-free error diffusion learning algorithms was accepted at ALIFE 2026 (Yamada et al., 2026) after limited human edits and verification.
3.
Cross-domain & harness validation spanning long-context LLM adaptation, evolutionary optimization, RL fine-tuning, and 5 additional domains. Across various tasks, model harnesses, and base models, our system managed to generate high-quality grounded research manuscripts.
4.
autoillustrate, an agentic pipeline for editable scientific figure generation that outperforms PaperBanana on PaperBananaBench  (Zhu et al., 2026a, 37.537.5 vs. 25.025.0 overall score,).
2 Background & Related Work
End-to-end automated scientific discovery.
Research on LLM-driven science increasingly targets complete workflows rather than isolated steps (Si et al., 2025).
The AI Scientist-v1 introduced a closed loop for proposing hypotheses, running experiments, analyzing results, and drafting manuscripts (Lu et al., 2024b), while The AI Scientist-v2 removed human-provided code templates and introduced progressive agentic tree search (Yamada et al., 2025). Ultimately, these developments combined with base LLM capabilities have led to end-to-end autonomous systems capable of producing workshop-level research content (Lu et al., 2026; Intology AI, 2025; AutoScience AI, 2025).
Agent Laboratory and AI-Researcher similarly connect literature analysis, experimentation, and report writing, with different opportunities for human feedback (Schmidgall et al., 2025; Tang et al., 2025).
CodeScientist combines literature and code search with code review, replication, and external assessment (Jansen et al., 2025), while CycleResearcher inserts automated review into the loop (Weng et al., 2025) and the AI co-scientist emphasizes scientist-guided hypothesis development (Gottweis et al., 2026).
These systems vary in autonomy, search, and validation; here our focus is the reusable plus scalable orchestration and evidence layer that supports heterogeneous coding agents.
Hill-Climbing via Program Evolution.
A complementary line casts discovery as search over executable artifacts.
FunSearch showed that language-model-guided program search can yield mathematical discoveries (Romera-Paredes et al., 2024); AlphaEvolve broadened evolutionary code search to algorithms and computational infrastructure (Novikov et al., 2025), and ShinkaEvolve targets open-ended, sample-efficient program evolution (Lange et al., 2026).
At a lighter weight, autoresearch uses coding agents to repeatedly edit and evaluate a training program under a fixed budget (Karpathy, 2026), motivating direct comparison with classical hyperparameter optimization (Ferreira et al., 2026). ALE-Agent (Imajuku et al., 2025) demonstrated that simple beam search at sufficient scale provides a competitive harness for program optimization.
Across these approaches, the search unit is a program under a pre-specified objective.
Our scaffold uses such executable search within a broader hypothesis-to-report process: it broadens the scope of application by automating task drafting, validation rubric generation, and supporting objective creation.
Benchmarking Research Agent Capabilities.
Research-agent benchmarks separate several capabilities that a single final score can obscure.
SWE-agent (Yang et al., 2024b) established that the agent–computer interface strongly affects real-world coding performance.
MLAgentBench evaluates iterative machine-learning experimentation (Huang et al., 2024), whereas MLE-Bench covers longer-horizon engineering workflows involving data preparation, debugging, training, and optimization (Chan et al., 2025); subsequent work studies how search strategy affects exploration and generalization in this setting (Toledo et al., 2025).
At the research level, PaperBench measures replication of published AI work (Starace et al., 2025), MLR-Bench targets open-ended machine-learning research (Chen et al., 2025), and EXP-Bench isolates whether agents can conduct valid AI experiments (Kon et al., 2026).
Lu et al. (2024b) introduced an LLM-driven pipeline for paper reviewing, which was later validated on research papers published after the training data cutoff date (Lu et al., 2026).
Here, we make use of such automated proxy AI reviews and additionally report external reviews of the co-produced ALIFE submission as complementary evidence.
Verification and Grounding.
Verification and communication are becoming explicit parts of agent design.
Rubric rewards can train AI co-scientists and provide feedback in domains without verifiable objectives (Goel et al., 2026; Gunjal et al., 2026).
Here, rubrics do not train the coding-agent backbone; instead, they serve as runtime contracts that ground each hypothesis to implementation checks and empirical evidence. The The Agentic Scientist manuscript drafting runs additional checks for facts and figures outlined in the paper. We discuss further related work in Appendix A.
3 The Agentic Scientist: An Orchestration Layer for Research Agents
The Agentic Scientist is a versatile agentic orchestrator scaffold for end-to-end computational research campaigns (Figure 1).
A campaign begins with a task specification, which researchers can provide directly or generate from a research brief with a drafting agent.
Then, the campaign proceeds through three phases: parallel investigation, retrospective evidence generation, and grounded paper writing.
Each phase runs as a separate process and feeds the next: the selected program and experimental artifacts produced by the first phase determine which baselines, ablations, and post-hoc analyses to run; the resulting evidence then grounds the manuscript. Finally, we leverage the previously introduced AI reviewer (Lu et al., 2026) to provide proxy evaluation scores for the manuscripts.
3.1 Research Task Setup
Before idea execution, The Agentic Scientist requires an executable task specification that defines the research goal and its evaluation. This can either be specified manually or drafted agentically:
Figure 2: Task drafting workflow.
Task specification.
A task specification comprises an initial program, a search evaluation program, an optional final evaluation program, and a machine-readable contract:
the initial program provides a deterministic baseline, while the search evaluation program determines whether a candidate program is correct, returns the scalar score used to rank and select candidates during the investigation phase, and records metrics and artifacts for later analysis.
When provided, the final evaluation program runs the selected program after exploration and reports post-search evaluation metrics.
The contract defines the candidate interface and evaluation procedures.
Tasks may additionally stage papers or code as research context, as in our hypernetwork adaptation and error-diffusion studies (Sections 6.1 and 4).
Task drafting.
Researchers may provide the task specification directly or use the task drafting pipeline to create it from a human-written research brief (Figure 2).
The pipeline has two main stages: a design agent converts the research brief into an experimental design, and a drafting agent converts this design into the task specification.
The experimental design defines the method, candidate interface, compute budget, and protocols for search and final evaluations.
Before drafting begins, an independent review agent assesses the design and repairs any issues.
The drafting agent then implements the initial program, the search and final evaluation programs, and the task contract, while pinning dependencies and staging the required data.
The harness checks the contract and runs smoke tests of the evaluation programs before the alignment judge assesses scientific fidelity and verifies that the search and final evaluators are independent and use disjoint data.
In containerized runs, the production check additionally requires the initial program to complete the full search workload to ensure operational feasibility.
Failed scaffolds are repaired, and only versions that pass all checks proceed to parallel investigation.
3.2 Parallel Investigation
The idea investigation phase spends a fixed budget of containerized research cycles across parallel workers, each exploring different hypotheses in a separate workspace while sharing distilled knowledge (Figure 3).
Multiple agent-based workers explore ideas in parallel, each advancing an independent sequence of research cycles.
These workers lease cycles from a synchronized queue, maintain separate run directories and worker-local best search score programs, which resemble a per-worker beam search.
A cycle starts with the worker importing the shared memory and the other workers’ distilled conclusions, and ends with the worker publishing new memory entries and updated conclusions to all other workers.
Communication occurs at cycle boundaries, while logs and program artifacts remain in worker directories.
At regular intervals, optional context pruning archives the histories and memory before resetting them, so that accumulated context does not bias subsequent cycles; the best program is carried into the next round to preserve progress.
After parallel execution, the aggregator merges worker histories and selects the best correct program found across workers. This selected program becomes the input to the retrospective evidence-generation phase.
3.3 Contracted Research Cycles
Figure 3: Investigation workflow.
Each worker advances a sequence of research cycles, and each cycle runs two agents in turn: an ideation agent and an execution agent.
The ideation agent turns one hypothesis into a typed contract, and the execution agent then tests that contract against the evaluator, so that intent and evidence remain separate(Figure 3).
Ideation agent.
At the start of a cycle, the ideation agent receives the task, recent idea and attempt histories, the manifest for the worker’s best program, and distilled conclusions.
The protocol requires inspecting this context without experimenting or modifying candidates (‘read-only’).
The ideation agent must instead emit a structured idea bundle containing a title, a falsifiable hypothesis, an ordered implementation plan, and the target rubrics.
Those records are normalized, and any missing fields or mismatched rubric identifiers are rejected.
This boundary records the idea as a structured artifact, so the execution agent does not have to reconstruct its intent from conversational context.
Execution agent.
The accepted contract, evaluator, task files, and the worker’s best program are staged into an experiment workspace.
The execution agent creates append-only candidate variants and follows an edit–evaluate–verify loop within a fixed evaluation budget, producing metrics, correctness checks, and rubric evidence.
Two distinct gates are then applied: an all-rubric-pass attempt marks the contract complete, whereas updating the worker’s best program requires a correct candidate with a strictly higher score; rubric completion and updates to the worker’s best program are therefore independent decisions.
All logged idea provenance and attempt outcomes are retained and later condensed to guide interpretation during paper writing.
3.4 Retrospective Evidence Generation
This phase studies the selected program from the investigation phase in depth: (1) it runs controlled baselines and ablations that isolate the idea effects, and (2) it derives post-hoc analyses that characterize the behavior of the method on the considered task.
Controlled baselines and ablations.
The selected program is first instrumented with additional metrics, such as extra scores and time series data, so that every generated baseline and ablation can be compared in detail on common ground.
Given the code diff between the selected and initial programs, an agent then proposes baselines and ablations with explicit hypotheses and implementation plans, where each ablation must isolate an innovation introduced by the selected program.
A separate agent filters these proposals for relevance, feasibility, and single-change scope.
Each validated plan receives an isolated workspace and follows an agent-guided edit–evaluate–verify loop that modifies the selected program to implement the intended change and score it against the evaluator, alongside the unchanged selected and initial programs.
The evaluator can be run for multiple seeds to make comparisons statistically meaningful, and their summaries and metrics are aggregated across seeds, ablations, and baselines by task.
Post-hoc analysis.
The post-hoc pipeline proposes, filters, executes, and aggregates diagnostics that characterize the behavior of the frozen selected program on the considered task.
Diagnostics may run new inference or analyze existing artifacts, such as checkpoints and logs.
Programmatic checks reject proposals that retrain or otherwise alter the selected program, or require unavailable artifacts.
When no usable checkpoint exists, an optional bootstrap stage enables checkpoint saving and reruns the evaluator once; all subsequent analyses keep the resulting version frozen.
The resulting post-hoc analyses, together with the baseline and ablation results, form the evidence that the paper writing phase turns into empirical claims.
3.5 Grounded Paper Writing
The paper writing phase converts the outputs of the preceding phases into a manuscript through an artifact-grounded workflow that reviews and audits claims made in the paper (Figure 4).
Figure 4: Evidence-to-paper pipeline.
Evidence staging and planning.
We merge worker results into aggregate outputs and stage them in a dedicated workspace, with a memory summary and raw artifacts.
Quantitative claims resolve to those outputs and raw artifacts, while the memory summary only guides interpretation.
Before and after drafting, reference planning retrieves bibliography entries (Section F.1). An optional stage fixes the central thesis, result emphasis, and figure plan.
Manuscript generation.
The writer agent produces a compilable LaTeX manuscript together with a claim-to-source map and figure manifest, whose plots are derived from staged workspace results.
The strategy may also request autoillustrate, our agentic figure generation pipeline (Section 3.6), to produce a conceptual overview diagram.
Validation and review.
At the end of our pipeline, a single repair loop alternates deterministic checks with agent judgment and feeds any failures back to the writer agent for revision.
The validation checks cover structural completeness, provenance and claim-to-source links, compilation, and venue and page budget constraints.
An independent audit agent runs every round on a read-only copy of the workspace and flags where the paper’s numbers, comparisons, or conclusions overclaim or contradict the canonical data and evidence trail.
An optional reviewer-style agent assesses framing, caveats, figures, and clarity, and may ask the writer agent to narrow or qualify claims.
3.6 autoillustrate: Conceptual Diagram Generation
autoillustrate is designed around two requirements: scientists should be able to modify conceptual figures, and they should represent the methodology faithfully.
To meet these requirements, the pipeline first produces an editable draw.io diagram to ground the figure’s structure, which is later exported and augmented using a separate image generation model.
As illustrated in Figure 5, the concept generator runs a six-phase pipeline that turns a method brief into a multi-panel figure whose layout source remains explicitly editable.
Unless stated otherwise, all papers generated by our system in this work have their main conceptual figure generated by autoillustrate.
Editable panel generation.
The pipeline begins with a planning agent that reads the staged source and instructions and describes the panels within one top-level configurable layout.
A styling agent then defines a shared color palette and typography specification to keep independently rendered panels consistent.
Rendering agents create the panels independently and in parallel as editable draw.io diagrams.
Each agent relies on a template to define the panel topology and a Python generator script to compute coordinates and check bounding boxes for overlap.
Visuals that draw.io cannot produce remain as placeholder boxes.
When possible, a generated matplotlib script fills these boxes; the enhancement stage handles any remaining placeholders.
A vision-model audit then evaluates each panel for rendering fidelity, factual accuracy, and sufficient detail.
When the inspection detects a defect, the pipeline edits the source, rebuilds the panel, and repeats the audit.
Composition, enhancement, and verification.
After all panels pass, the pipeline composes them into a single editable draw.io source and exports a raster version.
An image generation model restyles the raster export, after which a final verification step compares the enhanced candidate with the editable reference and repeats the checks defined during panel inspection.
Any discrepancy triggers another source-editing, export, enhancement, and verification pass.
After several repair passes, the pipeline returns the enhanced candidate with the strongest verification results.
If no candidate passes verification, the pipeline returns the raster export from the editable source.
Because the pipeline preserves this source throughout the process, scientists can revise the content or layout directly without regenerating the figure.
We provide additional details in Appendix E.
Figure 5: autoillustrate pipeline, generated by autoillustrate itself. (A) Six phases turn a document and an instruction into an editable draw.io figure: LLM sub-agents decide content, while deterministic Python owns geometry and the generative image model only restyles. (B) In Phase 2, panels are rendered from a combination of automatically-generated templates whose coordinates are determined by LLM-generated Python code. (C) In Phases 5–6, the enhanced candidate with the best audit results is selected; if none is acceptable, the deterministic render is used as a fallback.
4 AI Co-Scientist and Conference Paper Submission
We validated a development version of our system within a co-scientist setting: The Agentic Scientist was used to automate parts of the research process targeting the extension of a backpropagation-free neural network training method.
After thorough human verification and manuscript edits, we submitted a single paper to ALIFE 2026, which was accepted after external peer review as a main conference talk.
We emphasize that this single submitted ALIFE paper was produced by human researchers using The Agentic Scientist as a tool, and not submitted as an unaltered system output. The human authors remain responsible for its methods, claims, authorship, and disclosure.
In this section, our focus is to describe the process by which experimental outputs became a submitted manuscript and were subsequently revised for the camera-ready version (Figure 6).
Figure 6: Camera-ready ALIFE 2026 paper after the single submitted manuscript was accepted. The full manuscript and additional details can be found in Appendix D.
4.1 A Human-Supervised Research Workflow
The project began with a human-selected research direction: investigating how Error Diffusion (ED) could be extended beyond its earlier demonstrations (Kaneko, 2000; Fujita, 2026). ED leverages a three-factor learning rule (presynaptic activity, postsynaptic activity, and a globally broadcast error signal) originally studied in a dual-stream setting with paired excitatory (E) and inhibitory (I) neurons. We chose to illuminate this research direction together with The Agentic Scientist since it investigates the following question: how can classical biologically-inspired learning algorithms be revived with modern deep learning techniques?
Human researchers specified the scientific objective and the constraints for a valid implementation.
Within that contract, The Agentic Scientist proposed candidates, implemented training procedures, ran experiments, inspected failures, and generated plots.
The human researchers selected benchmarks (see creation process overview in Figure 7) and specified initial rubrics:
A numerically strong result was not treated as evidence of progress unless its implementation also satisfied the rubric constraints.
For example, one rubric prohibited hidden credit assignment through automatic differentiation or transposed forward weights. This required the positive and negative streams to remain distinct dual-stream, and specified the intended local information available to an update.
Figure 7: Creation process of the ALIFE paper: human researchers defined the initial direction, baseline, and rubrics; supported the experiment domain transition; verified results, references, and claims before submission; and incorporated reviewer feedback into the camera-ready paper.
4.2 Summary of Discovery and Dynamics
The Agentic Scientist first extended ED from binary to multiclass classification: during the parallel investigation phase, The Agentic Scientist developed modulo error routing, a structured mechanism for assigning multi-class output errors to hidden units.
This enabled multiclass learning while preserving the dual-stream, Dale-constrained architecture, in which weight signs remain fixed throughout training.
Subsequent experiments focused on robustifying learning dynamics: The Agentic Scientist identified layer-specific sigmoid widths, batch-centered error signals, and asymmetric E/I initialization as important stabilizers, raising accuracy to 96.7%96.7\% on MNIST (LeCun et al., 1998) and 61.7%61.7\% on CIFAR-10 (Krizhevsky et al., 2009).
Ablations further showed that the individual contributions were task-dependent: widened sigmoids were critical on MNIST, whereas batch-centering became substantially more important on CIFAR-10.
At this stage, human researchers transferred the best-performing ED implementation from the MNIST and CIFAR-10 experiments to the RL setting, and prepared the Brax (Freeman et al., 2021) and Craftax (Matthews et al., 2024) environments.
For RL experiments, an additional methodological constraint was added to the rubrics: the method was required to retain the PPO objective (Schulman et al., 2017), while using the gradient of the PPO loss with respect to the logits as the output-level error signal for Error Diffusion.
Within this constraint, The Agentic Scientist adapted the dual-stream ED mechanism to PPO and explored the resulting ED-PPO variants.
ED-PPO achieved performance comparable to standard baselines on several Brax tasks, although its performance was less consistent on Humanoid and Craftax (see figures in ALIFE paper manuscript; Section C.3).
Post-hoc analyses linked these results to the dynamics of credit assignment: local update signals weakened substantially in earlier layers, while training shifted E/I ratios toward balance.
In summary, the results obtained demonstrate that ED generalizes beyond its original binary-classification setting. The mechanisms required for effective learning depend on task difficulty and domain.
4.3 Human Result Verification & Manuscript Edits
Before submission to ALIFE, researchers verified the generated research artifacts.
This included checking that the generated implementations matched the scientific hypotheses being tested, confirming that the reported tables and figures accurately reflected results produced by the corresponding code, and revising the manuscript where the generated text overstated or inaccurately characterized the evidence.
For example, human researchers added appropriate statistical analyses and, when the results showed comparable performance, revised claims such as “ED-PPO significantly exceeds BP-PPO” to “ED-PPO performs on par with BP-PPO”.
Additionally, all references in the BibTeX bibliography were manually verified to ensure that the cited works existed and supported the statements for which they were cited.
During this process, human researchers removed several citations that did not support the corresponding claims, and added important references omitted from the generated manuscript.
These included the reference to the original ED work, which is available only through an archived webpage.
The generated manuscript was treated as an intermediate research artifact, which was modified by human researchers, improving the writing of several sections (e.g., future work, etc.). We then submitted this single paper to the ALIFE 2026 conference.
