4.4 Peer Review, Camera-Ready Paper Revisions
This submission process illustrates the AI co-scientist approach enabled by our system: outputs were not just optimized code or isolated experimental reports, but a coherent working paper making a scientific claim, positioning it relative to prior work, and presenting evidence across multiple domains. We emphasize that only a single paper was submitted after our careful manual verification.
The subsequent ALIFE peer review process provides external evidence for the quality of the co-created paper submission.
Overall, the paper was accepted as a talk, with individual recommendations of Strong ACCEPT, Weak REJECT, and Weak ACCEPT:
Reviewer 1 framed the work as a relevant artificial-life contribution, as it evaluates the consequences of a biological assumption, Dale’s principle, and found the methods and results technically sound.
Reviewer 2 highlighted that the neuroscience connection of the work was interesting but questioned whether the paper would better fit a neural network venue. Additionally, they asked for clearer explanations of notation, matrix conventions, modulo indexing, and technical details.
Reviewer 3 was positive and requested more intuition about ED updates, non-negative activations, per-neuron error signals, baseline comparisons, ED-PPO architecture, and the meaning of gradient attenuation without backpropagation.
We afterward used this feedback to enhance the camera-ready version of the manuscript: the post-acceptance changes are best characterized as targeted scientific clarifications rather than a change in the underlying study.
In particular, the camera-ready version tightens several claims about prior ED results, adds Dale-compliant reinforcement-learning results, corrects the Error Diffusion citation context, clarifies that Direct Feedback Alignment (Nøkland, 2016) routes errors through fixed random matrices, and cleans up the dual-stream architecture description and ED update equation.
It also replaces potentially misleading gradient language with “proxy” or “local surrogate” gradient terminology, refines the biological interpretation of E/I balance, repositions the discussion of Craftax and the dual-stream parameter overhead, and fixes typographic details such as quotation marks.
These edits make the paper more precise about biological plausibility and local learning while leaving the empirical evidence and overall narrative intact.
Table 2 summarizes human intervention throughout the manuscript-development process.
In summary, this study provides evidence of The Agentic Scientist being capable of supporting researchers to explore unorthodox AI topics across the entire research process.
5 Evaluating The Agentic Scientist Across Tasks, Harnesses, and LLMs
We evaluate The Agentic Scientist across five manually prepared research tasks that span optimization, representation learning, and predictive modelling. Each campaign starts from a task specification with different research objectives, starting points, and search iteration resources (see Table 3 for the task suite overview).
Task, harness, and model sweep.
We run the task sweep across four agent harnesses: Codex, Claude Code, Pi, and OpenCode (OpenAI, 2026; Anthropic, 2026; Earendil Works, 2026; Anomaly Innovations, 2026). Codex and Claude Code each use four assigned models, while Pi and OpenCode each use three, all at high reasoning effort (Table 10). Each run uses 20 idea investigation iterations and concludes with a written manuscript. Here, we focus on aggregated insights and provide more details on task-specific settings and discoveries can be found in Section B.1.
Table 3: Research tasks, starting implementations, and objectives used in the aggregate comparison.
Task
Starting Point
Research Objective
Learned optimization
Baseline: ELO’s expert-supervised MLP optimizer, meta-trained on (Fashion-)MNIST (Huang et al., 2026b); Evaluation: CIFAR-10 and SVHN.
Improve transfer to unseen datasets and network architectures under a fixed meta-training budget. Search iteration budget: 2 H100s, 150 min.
World models
Baseline: Action-free Pong model, with a trained discrete tokenizer and masked-token dynamics; Evaluation: Error reduction against TinyWorlds
Reduce held-out free-running rollout error across prediction horizons relative to Majmudar (2026). Search iteration budget: 4 H100s, 180 min.
Sparse autoencoders (SAE)
Baseline: TopK SAE activation-trained on Pythia- 160M-deduped & Gemma-2-2B-it; Evaluation: Task-specific SAEBench (Karvonen et al., 2025).
Improve reconstruction at fixed sparsity while preserving model behavior and feature utilization. Search iteration budget: 2 H100s, 60 min.
