Improve sample-efficient optimization over continuous, mixed, and hierarchical search spaces. Search iteration budget: 8 CPU cores, 30 min.
Paper-level evaluation protocol.
All generated research runs share the same scoring function within a task instance and can thereby be directly compared on their numerical optimization outcome. Additionally, we use the AI Reviewer’s (Lu et al., 2026) 1–10 Overall score as a paper-level proxy metric:
For each generated manuscript, five separate calls to o4-mini-2025-04-16 review the extracted PDF text using NeurIPS-style guidelines and a fixed in-context example of a paper–review pair. A sixth call acts as an area chair to synthesize the reviews.
The original study in Lu et al. (2026) validated the reviewer against acceptance decisions for ICLR submissions from OpenReview, including a post-training-data-cutoff 2025 cohort.
We note that the AI reviewer score solely serves as a proxy for reviewer-perceived paper quality, and not as ground-truth evidence of full scientific correctness.
Model capability, harness impact, and paper quality.
Generally speaking, newer model releases tend to be associated with higher reviewer scores (Figure 8a). When evaluating the models within their respective native coding harnesses, this observation appears to be more strongly pronounced for Claude- than GPT-based models.
Matched-model comparisons show minor performance differences across harness choice, while the underlying model matters more (Figure 8b). No harness is uniformly best. OpenCode ranks lowest with GPT-5.6-Sol but highest with Opus 5 and DeepSeek-v4-Flash. The model developers’ own harnesses offer no consistent advantage: Codex ties Pi with GPT-5.6-Sol, and Claude Code falls between OpenCode and Pi with Opus 5.
Figure 8: Pre-revision reviewer scores by (a) model release date and (b) harness, averaged across five tasks; (c) within-task percentile ranks of final performance and reviewer scores, colored by task. The least-squares fit uses a pointwise 95% band from 10,000 bootstrap resamples of the 14 complete harness–model profiles across all five tasks, conditional on the observed tasks and fixed ranks.
Search progress and rubric dynamics.
The search traces show gains in best observed scores alongside fluctuating rubric pass rates. Final performance is positively associated with reviewer scores on four of the five tasks, most strongly for hyperparameter optimization, while learned optimization shows little association (Figure 9).
Pooling all 70 campaigns after converting both final performance and reviewer scores to within-task percentile ranks gives a positive aggregated association (r=0.44r=0.44; Figure 8c).
This observation is consistent with manuscript reviewer scores tracking measured numerical performance.
Figure 9: Search scores (top), rubric pass rates (middle), and final performance versus reviewer score (bottom) across five tasks. Bottom panels show within-task performance percentiles against original 1–10 reviewer scores; annotated least-squares slopes give reviewer-score points per 10 percentile points. Final performance is positively associated with reviewer scores on four of the five tasks.
6 Additional Case Studies: Putting The Agentic Scientist to work
We further evaluate The Agentic Scientist on three case studies: Language model adaptation (Section 6.1) through hypernetworks (Charakorn et al., 2026), evolutionary optimization (Section 6.2) through meta-learned neural network parametrizations (Lange et al., 2023a), and language model RL fine-tuning (Section 6.3) on mathematical reasoning tasks.
6.1 Open-Ended Architecture Research with The Agentic Scientist:  Discovering Query-Conditioned Composition for Hypernetwork-Based LLM Adaptation
In this experiment, we explore how The Agentic Scientist can autonomously write an insightful scientific paper after successfully investigating a specific topic. As a case study, we choose the recently published hypernetwork-based LLM adaptation work from Charakorn et al. (2026), Doc-to-LoRA. Unlike the other settings, The Agentic Scientist is given the Doc-to-LoRA paper’s LaTeX source and its public code repository as the starting point.
Figure 10: (Left) Search trajectories for Doc-to-LoRA scientist runs given 40 idea iteration budget with Codex + GPT-5.6-Sol, Claude + Opus-5, and Pi + DeepSeek-V4-Pro on the multi-NIAH task. Both Codex and Claude runs improve steadily on the task. (Right) Exact-match accuracy is shown as a heatmap grouped by the number of test sample needles. Red crosses indicate invalid iterations.
Problem formulation.
Doc-to-LoRA introduces a hypernetwork that outputs low-rank adaptation (LoRA) matrices for a target LLM given a textual document. The generated LoRA serves as a weight-distilled knowledge bank and can be used to update the target LLM for document-based question answering without the need to place the documents in the LLM’s context window.
To handle documents beyond the context length of its encoder model, Doc-to-LoRA splits long documents into chunks and generates one LoRA independently for each chunk.
The standard approach concatenates the per-chunk low-rank factors into a higher-rank LoRA. However, naively combining multiple LoRAs can cause interference between several facts induced by different chunks.
Here, we focus on multi-needle-in-a-haystack (multi-NIAH) retrieval as a proxy task: each context contains one to four named facts, but the prompt asks for only one target needle’s four-digit number. To be successful at this task, a trained hypernetwork must avoid multi-LoRA interference via compositional LoRA designs.
Needle-in-a-haystack task setup.
Any modification proposed by The Agentic Scientist is limited to architectural or training-related edits given a fixed encoder Gemma-2-2b-it model (Gemma Team, 2024), fixed data generation, fixed evaluation scripts, and a 3-hour single-GPU training time budget.
The evaluation spans five context-length bins from short up to 65,53665{,}536 tokens, with needles randomly placed at random positions. Given the resulting Doc-2-LoRA weights, θ\theta, the investigation target for The Agentic Scientist is the exact-match accuracy on the queried number:
maxθ⁡score​(θ)=1|D|​∑d∈DEMd⁡(θ);D={128, 8192, 16384, 32768, 65536},\max_{\theta}\;\text{score}(\theta)=\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{d\in D}\operatorname{EM}_{d}(\theta);\qquad D=\{128,\ 8192,\ 16384,\ 32768,\ 65536\},
where EM is exact-match accuracy and DD is the set of evaluation context-length bins.
The Agentic Scientist search dynamics.
We ran The Agentic Scientist for 40 idea-investigation steps with three model-harness combinations (Figure 10).
The Claude and Codex variants both reach near-optimal scores, with Claude improving faster.
In contrast, Pi + DeepSeek-v4-Pro’s run shows fewer but larger jumps and plateaus below 50%.
In the Claude run, the generated report organizes the proposals by the mechanism each one tests: architecture redesign, auxiliary training objectives, LoRA merge weighting, and query-conditioned composition.
The key discoveries are as follows: redesigning the architecture or adding auxiliary objectives yields little gain, whereas query-conditioning the LoRA composition alone raises accuracy to 90.8%90.8\%. Adding query-relevance filtering with delimiter-aligned chunk boundaries reaches 98.0%98.0\% (Figure 11).
The generated paper therefore concludes that the bottleneck is not adapter-generation capacity or the training loss: multi-needle Doc-to-LoRA retrieval is a routing problem, in which the system must decide, based on the query, which chunk’s adapter to activate when merging multiple adapters.
The full paper written by The Agentic Scientist and its AI review are presented in Section C.1.
(a) Exact-match accuracy by mechanism family.
(b) Ablations of the proposed components.
Figure 11: Doc-to-LoRA composition mechanism ablations figure in the generated report (Section C.1). (a) Hypernetwork’s head redesign, auxiliary objective, and unconditional merging stay at or below the 51.7%51.7\% exact-match accuracy, while query-conditioned composition reaches 98.0%98.0\%. (b) Starting from the query-blind (unconditional) merge, query-dependent merging provides a significant improvement; relevance-based text placement and delimiter-aligned chunking close the remaining gap.
Discovered Inductive Biases. The Agentic Scientist demonstrates that naively composing every generated adapter makes the target LLM conflate multiple facts. The main failure mode is responding with a distractor number rather than the queried one. The successful idea is to make the adapter merging query-conditioned: The Agentic Scientist proposes an interaction score between the query and each chunk that selects the right adapter at composition time, without adding trainable parameters or changing the training objective.
The Agentic Scientist then adds two smaller but complementary structural changes: relevance-based text processing and delimiter-aligned text chunking.
Relevance-based text processing removes the noise tokens around the token span most relevant to the query, so the adapter is generated from the queried fact, instead of the neighboring haystack.
Delimiter-aligned text chunking keeps each needle sentence intact before the query-conditioned address is computed.
We note that the The Agentic Scientist demonstrated that this inductive bias works well with the multi-needle-in-a haystack task. Future work should investigate the generalization of the discovered mechanisms beyond this synthetic long-context retrieval task.
6.2 Open-Ended Algorithm Discovery with The Agentic Scientist:
Meta-Evolving CMA-Anchored Learned Evolution Strategies
Black-box optimization (BBO) is an established target for automating algorithm discovery (Ma et al., 2025): compact update rules determine sampling distributions, solution recombination, and covariance adaptation. But coordinating these mechanisms remains difficult (Shala et al., 2020).
One line of prior work focused on learning attention-parametrized evolutionary algorithms (EA) through meta-BBO (Lange et al., 2023a; Lange et al., 2023b; Faldor et al., 2025a).
Here, we ask whether The Agentic Scientist can improve the meta-optimizer program itself (i.e., perform ‘meta-meta-BBO’).
Task setup.
The goal of this research campaign is to design a better procedure for obtaining a learned evolutionary algorithm.
Each program proposed by The Agentic Scientist is judged by how well its learned EA generalizes to unseen tasks. Evaluation proceeds in two steps: (1) the EA’s neural network parameters are meta-trained on a set of synthetic tasks, and (2) the resulting frozen parameters are evaluated on a separate held-out task.
For meta-training, we use CMA-ES (Hansen and Ostermeier, 2001): at each generation, a random subset of BBOB  (Hansen et al., 2021) tasks is sampled, and the program runs optimization for a population of EA parameters. The best task-specific objective values reached by an EA instance are aggregated into a single meta-training score.
After meta-training completes, the final CMA-ES mean is frozen and evaluated on a validation task: the learned EA algorithm evolves a linear policy on a robotic control task (Freeman et al., 2021, Brax Ant-v1,).
This evaluation tests the generalization capabilities to new tasks and optimization budgets (2,000 generations with a population size of 256). The agent-level search score is defined as:
score=(AUCcandidate−AUCinitial)/(AUCCMA​-​ES−AUCinitial),\mathrm{score}=\left(\mathrm{AUC}_{\mathrm{candidate}}-\mathrm{AUC}_{\mathrm{initial}}\right)/\left(\mathrm{AUC}_{\mathrm{CMA\text{-}ES}}-\mathrm{AUC}_{\mathrm{initial}}\right),
where AUC is the mean of the 2,000-point best-so-far return curve.
The synthetic tasks are exclusively used to train the weights of an The Agentic Scientist EA proposal, while the validation Ant evaluation measures task generalization and scores optimizer programs across agent iterations.
Figure 12:
Search trajectories for 30 idea iterations with GPT-5.6-Sol + Codex, Opus-5 + Claude, DeepSeek V4 Flash + Pi, and Kimi K3 + OpenCode.
Dots are individual Ant search-validation evaluations and step curves are the cumulative best.
The Agentic Scientist search dynamics.
All four considered The Agentic Scientist configurations obtain a normalized search score above one, indicating that each achieves a higher AUC than CMA-ES on the Ant-v1 task (Figure 12).
Although the final GPT-5.6-sol + Codex and Kimi K3 + OpenCode candidates improve AUC over CMA-ES, their final Ant-v1 returns remain low:
Opus-5 + Claude and DeepSeek V4 Flash + Pi achieve the highest AUCs and terminal returns, whereas Kimi K3 yields the smallest AUC gain.
Claude’s highest-scoring candidate exceeds the dual-timescale diagonal innovation-memory strategy discovered by Codex in both AUC and terminal return. Next, we present the discoveries made by The Agentic Scientist using the previous Claude + Opus 5 setup. The detailed The Agentic Scientist paper is available in Section C.2.
Strategy selected for the generated paper.
The system discovered CMA-Anchored LearnedES, which uses a separable-CMA (Ros and Hansen, 2008) distribution x=m+σ​d⊙zx=m+\sigma d\odot z and additionally augments the algorithm with three small shared MLPs containing 364 meta-evolved parameters:
A permutation-equivariant Deep Sets (Zaheer et al., 2017) module adjusts CMA recombination weights; global gates modulate mean and cumulative-step-size updates; and a coordinate-shared gate modulates diagonal covariance adaptation.
All learned outputs are bounded multiplicative residuals in [12,2][\frac{1}{2},2]; zero network outputs recover the separable-CMA update equations before the fixed sampling and re-expansion mechanisms described below.
The agent additionally implements fixed, horizon-free mechanisms around these learned gates (see Section C.2). These mechanisms are program structure rather than meta-evolved weights. Notably, the re-expansion cannot fire during the 50-generation BBOB inner loop, indicating an interplay between Ant-v1 validation and idea investigation.
Post-search transfer across Brax tasks.
The manuscript drafted by The Agentic Scientist mentions the possibility of limited meta-transfer to some unseen tasks. Therefore, we test the selected Claude, Codex, and Pi programs on eight Brax-v1 environments using 16 new shared seeds: seven unseen environments and Ant as the search-validation reference environment.
The task setup differs from the meta-held-out setting: the test evaluation uses a zero-initialized affine–tanh policy, population 256, 2,000 generations, initial standard deviation 0.05, and 500-step episodes.
Figure 13: Post-search evaluation across eight Brax-v1 legacy-spring environments.
Curves show the mean best-so-far return over 16 shared seeds; shaded regions denote one standard error.
The grey Ant panel marks the search-validation reference environment, evaluated here with new seeds and the reduced transfer-policy interface; the other seven environments were unseen during agentic search.
While no method dominates on all environments (Figure 13; Table 5)
the Opus-5 + Claude Code candidate matches or exceeds the mean terminal return of CMA-ES on 6 out of 8 environments and falls short on HalfCheetah and Hopper.
The DeepSeek V4 Flash + Pi candidate has the highest sample mean on HalfCheetah, but its margin over CMA-ES is smaller than the Pi candidate’s standard error.
The GPT-5.6-Sol + Codex candidate leads on Reacher and Walker2d.
We have released the discovered CMA-Anchored LearnedES optimizer for further study in evosax v0.3.2 (Lange, 2023).
6.3 RL-Based LLM Fine-Tuning with The Agentic Scientist:
Studying the Effects of Agent Choice, Parallelism, and Shared Knowledge
Finally, we also evaluate The Agentic Scientist as a hill-climbing system for Reinforcement Learning (RL)-based language model fine-tuning.
Additionally, we provide several ablations on key configuration settings of The Agentic Scientist system.
In this setting, the system iteratively improves a training program through algorithmic modifications and hyperparameter tuning.
Task setup.
The task consists of optimizing the training procedure of a Qwen2.5-Math model (Yang et al., 2024a) on the MATH dataset from Hendrycks et al. (2021).
We follow the task formulation of Si et al. (2026), in which each worker is initialized from a baseline Group Relative Policy Optimization  (Shao et al., 2024, GRPO,) training pipeline.
Our implementation is a re-engineered version of their reference code, restructured to integrate with the The Agentic Scientist sandbox, logging, and evaluation interfaces; the learning algorithm itself remains a faithful instantiation of GRPO.
Figure 14: Post-search test accuracy of the best program of each GRPO run.
Each evaluation selects a run’s best program using the fixed validation set, evaluates it on the held-out test set across five seeds, and reports the mean and standard deviation.
Search and post-search evaluation.
The Agentic Scientist (Opus-4.7 + Claude) optimizes the accuracy on a validation set for 50 idea investigation iterations. During this search it never has access to the test set.
For each completed run, we keep the program with the highest validation accuracy, and rerun it on the full training and validation sets together, and then perform post-search evaluation on the test set (Figure 14).
As shown in Figure 15, The Agentic Scientist iteratively improves validation accuracy from the GRPO baseline (≈ 52.7%{\approx}\,52.7\%) up to ≈ 71.9%{\approx}\,71.9\% in the best run.
In the post-search evaluation (Figure 14), the best program of each run reaches up to 68.0%68.0\% accuracy, well above the 47.8%47.8\% of the initial program.
The aggressive hyperparameters selected by the best two-worker variant occasionally cause training to diverge during post-search evaluation, suggesting that they can overfit to validation outcomes and favor configurations whose gains do not transfer reliably to independent retraining.
Algorithmic changes in the best programs.
We find that the improvements discovered by The Agentic Scientist combine real algorithmic changes with hyperparameter tuning: The Agentic Scientist re-implements and combines several known techniques from the recent literature on RL fine-tuning.
These are not independent rediscoveries: The Agentic Scientist had in principle access to these techniques through web search, and they likely appeared in the underlying model’s training data.
The most common ones are the DAPO “clip-higher” rule (Yu et al., 2025), an asymmetric PPO clip with a larger upper bound, so that promising low-probability tokens are updated more strongly;
Dr. GRPO loss aggregation (Liu et al., 2025), which divides the loss by a fixed constant instead of the response length and thus removes the bias against long responses;
and GSPO-style importance ratios (Zheng et al., 2025), that is, a single length-normalized ratio per response instead of one per token.
Smaller changes include removing or simplifying the KL penalty, keeping only the prompt groups with mixed correct and incorrect answers, and penalizing overly long responses.
Overall, these changes show that hill-climbing over an RL fine-tuning program can not only tune hyperparameters but also select, re-implement, and combine relevant algorithmic ideas from the recent literature.
The Agentic Scientist uses empirical feedback to decide which of these ideas to keep and how to combine them into training programs with higher accuracy.
Influence of the number of workers.
A recurring design choice across our experiments is the number of parallel workers spawned by the idea investigation stage of The Agentic Scientist at each iteration.
We study its effect on the RL fine-tuning task, running the system with 1, 2, and 4 parallel workers.
All worker configurations (Figure 15, Left) improve over the GRPO baseline validation accuracy (≈ 52.7%{\approx}\,52.7\%), with the 2-worker configuration reaching a validation accuracy of ≈ 66%{\approx}\,66\%.
Using multiple workers speeds up progress; for instance, the 2-worker variant reaches a score comparable to the 1-worker peak in less than half the wall-clock time, and effectively shares information between workers (Figure 15, Right).
Nonetheless, the benefit of parallelism is non-monotonic: using more workers does not necessarily yield better final results, suggesting that a small amount of parallel exploration injects useful diversity while larger pools can redundantly concentrate on similar local improvements.
Figure 15: Search dynamics of The Agentic Scientist on RL fine-tuning across three seeds per worker configuration.
Left: per-evaluation scores against iteration index and wall-clock duration.
Right: the combined 1- and 2-worker search tree per task, with examples of cross-worker inspirations relayed through shared memory. Small amounts of parallelism improve wall clock time and effectively transfer knowledge across workers. Further scaling of the parallelism does not show improvements.
Cross-agent comparison.
A central question is how much performance comes from the orchestration introduced by The Agentic Scientist versus the underlying coding agent. While we partially address this question in Section 5, we additionally compare Opus-4.7 + Claude Code, GPT-5.6-Sol + Codex, and Kimi K3 + Pi for the RL-based LLM fine-tuning task on the idea investigation phase in isolation.
Under this common setting, Pi achieves scores similar to Claude in a faster and more consistent manner, while reducing the API costs by 75%, and producing programs that generalize better to the post-search test dataset (Figure 16).
Codex, on the other hand, followed the task prompt instructions (Section G.5) more strictly: it never tuned hyperparameters after algorithmic changes, limiting the improvement process.
Overall, we conclude that the base model can have a significant impact on the idea investigation phase, including both final performance and search costs.
Rubric-specification sensitivity.
We observe a failure mode introduced by structural limitations of rubric-based verification: rubrics check only the criteria they enumerate, so they cannot catch violations of constraints that were never stated, and human researchers remain responsible for specifying tasks precisely and verifying that results respect their full intent.
In two of the three Pi runs, we observed that the agent also modified the test-time evaluation procedure itself (e.g., using kk-sample majority voting instead of the baseline’s pass@1), which inflates the reported validation accuracy without improving the training algorithm.
The task prompt asks the agent to improve the “training” algorithm, but never explicitly requires a fixed evaluation procedure. No initial rubric item covers this evaluation-protocol integrity. Neither the evaluator nor the rubric gates flagged these programs, and we identified them after a manual post-hoc audit.
After excluding protocol-violating programs, Pi still reaches high scores faster and more consistently than all other agents.
Figure 16: Cross-agent comparison under the same orchestration scaffold across three seeds per agent.
Left to Right: score evolution during the search, test performance after reevaluation, and cumulative API cost per evolution cycle.
Crosses mark evaluations whose program modified the evaluation protocol (identified by manual audit); they are excluded from all running-best curves.
Influence of shared knowledge.
A second central design choice is the shared knowledge that connects all workers to a common memory of prior results, run summaries, and failure traces (Section 3.2).
To isolate its contribution, we compare our default 2-worker The Agentic Scientist to an ablated no shared knowledge variant, where each worker cannot see other workers’ conclusions and memory.
Shared knowledge improves the search process: the default configuration progresses faster, achieves a higher mean best-program score, and reduces the performance gap between workers (Figure 17, centre and right).
Nonetheless, we observe a substantial amount of inter-run variance.
The two variants reach similar final best scores across workers: the default configuration scores slightly higher, but the difference is not statistically significant (Figure 17, left).
Hence, independent workers can find strong programs without cooperation at the cost of slower overall runtime.
These results suggest that sharing successful ideas accelerates the search and allows lower-scoring workers to benefit from improvements found by others.
Figure 17: Effect of shared knowledge on 2-worker GRPO search across three seeds per configuration. Crosses mark evaluations from the no-shared-knowledge runs whose program modified the evaluation protocol (identified by manual audit); they are excluded from all running-best curves.
7 Evaluating autoillustrate on PaperBananaBench
Figure 18: autoillustrate applied to the Darwin Gödel Machine paper (Zhang et al., 2026a): the editable diagram (left) and its corresponding enhanced figure (right).
Given a methodology description, autoillustrate produces an editable draw.io diagram and an enhanced figure derived from it (Figure 18).
We evaluate the generated figures on PaperBananaBench (Zhu et al., 2026a), following the original evaluation protocol and using GPT-6-Astra as the VLM judge, as it more reliably penalizes small structural errors such as disconnected arrows (Figure 22).
Results with Gemini-3.1-Pro as the judge are reported in Section E.2.
Each generated diagram is compared to its human-designed equivalent with respect to four criteria: Faithfulness, Readability, Conciseness, and Aesthetics.
Each comparison receives 100 points when the generated diagram wins, 50 for a tie, and 0 when it loses.
The Overall score prioritizes fidelity and clarity over brevity and visual polish: a method wins overall if it wins at least one of Faithfulness and Readability without losing the other; if neither wins, Conciseness and Aesthetics are used to break the tie.
Benchmark results.
The Claude-based autoillustrate pipeline achieves the highest Overall score, 37.5 against 25.0 for the best PaperBanana variant, driven by higher Faithfulness (Table 4).
All autoillustrate variants generate on average more faithful figures, which is crucial for automated research since it forms the basis of method communication.
PaperBanana produces overly detailed diagrams, with higher chances of small image-generation errors.
Conciseness is more challenging for autoillustrate, as the editable diagram primitives constrain compact and complex visual abstraction generation.
Although autoillustrate remains below the human-reference Overall score of 50.0, its editable source files allow users to refine generated diagrams.
The Codex-based variant is on par with the Claude-based pipeline on Faithfulness and Conciseness, but scores lower on Readability and Aesthetics, as it tends to generate more cluttered diagrams.
Removing the panel inspections and final verification critics lowers Faithfulness and Overall scores, as these audits detect and repair factual inaccuracies with respect to the source methodology.
Limitations: cost and runtime.
The autoillustrate variants generally have higher median API costs per diagram than PaperBanana, assuming raw API pricing.
Additionally, autoillustrate’s median runtime is higher, as producing editable draw.io diagrams requires agents to write XML templates and Python programs that place elements, avoid collisions, and route arrows precisely, which demands substantial reasoning.
Within autoillustrate, removing the critics approximately halves runtime, and using low inference effort reduces runtime by a further 30%.
