8 Conclusion
Summary.
Across multiple research case studies, The Agentic Scientist demonstrates that coding agents can contribute to scientific research when embedded in an orchestrated workflow that preserves evidence and tests claims.
Our results span several levels of scientific autonomy, including a co-created Error Diffusion paper accepted at a conference, the fully autonomous discovery of structural inductive biases for Doc-to-LoRA, and a concrete CMA-anchored learned optimizer.
Complementing these detailed case studies, our cross-harness and model evaluation comprises 70 research campaigns across five manually prepared tasks and 14 harness-model configurations. The manuscript AI reviews show differences across model generations, and a positive trend toward newer models. With the same LLM base model, we observe no consistent differences across harnesses. Stronger task search performance predicts higher AI reviewer scores on 4 of 5 considered tasks.
This positions The Agentic Scientist as a human-steerable scaffold for end-to-end computational research.
Discussion.
The Agentic Scientist and its experiments raise questions about the nature of scientific contributions on several levels: for the researcher, what to specify and how much autonomy to grant; for the generated artifacts, what a search score misses and how to validate generated research. We discuss each below:
From implementation to specification.
As coding agents take over implementation, the human role shifts toward specifying the research contract: the task, its evaluators, and the rubrics that gate each stage.
This contract is also where failures can arise.
Agents may exploit what the contract leaves unstated: AI Scientist-v1 edited its code to extend its own time limits (Lu et al., 2024b), and The Agentic Scientist changed the evaluation protocol on a subset of RL fine-tuning runs (Figure 16).
Furthermore, agents can follow the contract instructions too literally (Section 6.3), requiring a subtle balance of scientific expression and concrete verifiability.
Since neither deviation is apparent from the reported numbers alone, precise specification and auditing become central research skills.
Autonomy spectrum.
The AI Scientist-v2 produced a fully AI-generated paper that passed peer review at a workshop before its pre-agreed withdrawal (Yamada et al., 2025). The Error Diffusion paper, on the other hand, resulted from using The Agentic Scientist as a tool within a co-scientist setting.
The two modes are complementary: fully autonomous campaigns cheaply screen many directions, while human steering turns the most promising ones into verified contributions.
In the Error Diffusion project, this human effort was supplied throughout: defining the direction, baseline, and initial rubrics; verifying results, references, and claims; adding reinforcement-learning experiments; and clarifying notation and mechanisms after peer review (Section 4).
Beyond hill-climbing.
A higher search score often does not by itself imply a better scientific contribution.
Although Figure 9 shows that higher task scores tend to come with higher AI reviewer scores, the score hides two aspects of scientific contributions.
First, a single search objective can hide trade-offs: a richer sparse autoencoder encoder reduces reconstruction error but worsens a feature-perturbation metric, and a tabular deployment recipe raises balanced accuracy while lowering ordinary accuracy.
Second, a score gain can come from low-level engineering rather than high-level innovation: in RL fine-tuning of language models, gains came from re-implementing and combining existing techniques rather than from new mechanisms (Section 6.3).
Post-search ablations, analysis, and reporting expose the first; the second requires a provenance map that links ideas, hypotheses, and claims to prior work, extending the current claim-to-source map beyond experimental evidence.
Validating generated research.
We evaluate research campaigns with several signals of different strength, each limited when viewed in isolation: AI reviewer scores, post-search transfer evaluations (such as unseen Brax environments in Section 6.2), and external peer review.
Peer review most directly assesses scientific merit but needs expert time for every manuscript, so it cannot cover all campaigns, while AI reviewer scores can potentially be overfit like any other metric.
Post-search transfer evaluations offer a scalable middle ground, provided they are fixed before search and held out from it.
Other signals could strengthen this evidence: Reviewers drawn from several model families to dilute the circularity of using the same models for investigation and rubric review (Zheng et al., 2023); independent reproduction experiments behind each claim; and a measure of idea novelty, which previous signals do not capture.
Limitations.
At present, The Agentic Scientist could be enhanced by a reliable literature-to-code pipeline for constructing state-of-the-art baselines.
The task drafting pipeline (Section 3.1) is also not fully robust: it can draft simple tasks such as circle packing from a research brief, but for larger tasks such as the world-model and tabular distribution-shift tasks in Section 5, it may fail to produce an initial program, evaluators, and task contract that exactly match the full Agentic Scientist scaffold.
Reliable task drafting nevertheless remains an important capability, since it removes the last manual step between a research brief and an automated campaign.
Unlike AI Scientist-v1 and AI Scientist-v2 (Lu et al., 2024b; Yamada et al., 2025), The Agentic Scientist only explicitly searches the literature during manuscript writing rather than during initial ideation. That being said, we repeatedly observe that coding agents leverage general web access to inform idea investigation.
Beyond these capability gaps, the system’s verification is only as complete as its task-specific evaluators and rubrics, which may omit methodological errors or reward unintended shortcuts.
Results that pass verification should therefore still be inspected and validated by human researchers.
Although the cross-harness and model evaluation covers 70 campaigns, its paper-level metric is an AI-reviewer proxy rather than full evidence of scientific correctness, and only the Error Diffusion paper underwent external peer review.
Our evaluations are also restricted to computational machine learning, so they do not establish the feasibility of transferring the orchestration workflow to domains requiring physical experiments or human-subject studies.
Finally, for autoillustrate we present additional component-specific limitations and failure cases in Section E.3.
Ethical considerations.
AI-assisted generation of research can create risks for attribution, research integrity, and an already constrained peer-review process.
We submitted only the single Error Diffusion paper for external review after human verification; the other generated manuscripts served as internal evaluation artifacts.
For the Error Diffusion paper specifically, human researchers verified its implementations, results, claims, and references, correcting unsupported claims and incomplete attribution before submission.
Responsible deployment also requires sandboxed execution, provenance for generated code and text, disclosure of agent contributions, watermarking of generated papers, and human accountability for safety, authorship, and final claims.
More broadly, researchers remain responsible for disclosing agent contributions and deciding whether generated work is suitable for submission and external review.
Future work.
Scientific progress often comes from exploring diverse “lottery tickets” across seemingly unrelated domains.
Applying The Agentic Scientist broadly could accelerate this process by screening a larger hypothesis space through greater compute within individual research directions and exploration of more directions within a topic.
To support human validation, the claim-to-source map should be exposed through an interface that lets researchers navigate from each claim to the experiments and results behind it.
The interface should also allow researchers to provide domain expertise and use the system as an interactive research tool.
Another improvement would be to automate the design of post-search transfer evaluations.
Where possible, rubric criteria should be implemented as automated tests, checked for reward hacking, and complemented by independent scientific review  (Goel et al., 2026; Gunjal et al., 2026).
With traceable evidence and enhanced rubrics, The Agentic Scientist could broaden and accelerate experimental search while leaving scientific judgment and responsibility with researchers.
