Table of Contents
Appendix A Additional Related Work
From fixed search spaces to long-running research agents.
Computational discovery has traditionally been posed as search through a hypothesis space chosen in advance by the system’s designers (Langley, 2024).
Expressing the search space as code relaxes this constraint.
Programs written by language models have specified the learning problem, as training objectives (Lu et al., 2024a), rewards (Ma et al., 2024), and environments (Faldor et al., 2025b), as well as the learner, as network architectures (Chen et al., 2023), robot designs (Lehman et al., 2023), and agent designs (Hu et al., 2025).
At the scale of a research project, AIDE runs tree search over machine-learning engineering code (Jiang et al., 2025), and AgentRxiv lets separate research agents build on one another’s results through a shared preprint server (Schmidgall and Moor, 2025).
Several concurrent systems push further, sustaining research over long horizons or running many agents on a single problem.
DeepScientist (Weng et al., 2026) and InternAgent-1.5 (Feng et al., 2026) run a long-horizon research loop over a cumulative memory, with DeepScientist promoting only findings that survive verification; InternAgent-1.5 and EvoScientist (Lyu et al., 2026) further distill what worked and what failed into reusable memories.
AutoScientists (Gao et al., 2026), CORAL (Qu et al., 2026a), and Agora (Zhang et al., 2026b) instead run many agents at once and coordinate them through shared state rather than a central planner: self-organizing teams around a shared experimental record, asynchronous agents in isolated workspaces with a separate evaluator, and workers appending results and verifications to a Git graph.
Our workers likewise explore in separate workspaces and exchange distilled conclusions at cycle boundaries; in addition, each cycle is bound to a typed contract, and the selected program is then studied through controlled baselines and ablations.
Judging ideas, rubrics, and evidence auditing.
Deciding which ideas and results to trust is a recurring difficulty.
Language models can propose ideas grounded in the literature (Wang et al., 2024a; Baek et al., 2025), and lightweight graph-based evaluators (Feng et al., 2025) or LLM judges that track human preferences (Zheng et al., 2023) can score them.
Other work anchors results to something checkable: RE-Bench compares agents with human experts on research engineering (Wijk et al., 2025), and data-to-paper traces every result in a manuscript back through the code to the data (Ifargan et al., 2025).
Final scores and papers can still conceal how they were obtained.
An independent evaluation of The AI Scientist found weak novelty checks, failed experiments, and poorly substantiated manuscripts behind its claims (Beel et al., 2025), and controlled audits of two open-source systems found biased benchmark selection, post-hoc selection on test results, and undisclosed subsampled or synthetic data, flaws that an LLM auditor detected far more reliably from logs and code than from the paper alone (Luo et al., 2026).
Closest to our contracted research cycles, Xcientist specifies at each stage the implementation, comparisons, and artifacts a claim requires and retains the source evidence behind it; a retrospective audit then measures claim drift, that is, unresolved mismatches between accepted claims and their sources, code, or experiments (Wang et al., 2026).
Verification can also be delegated to models: Gemini has been used to hunt for flaws in proofs and to write and run code that checks derivations (Woodruff et al., 2026), and auto-generated rubrics have served as the reward for post-training an AI scientist agent on paper replication (Falck et al., 2026).
In The Agentic Scientist, the ideation agent fixes the target rubrics before execution, and retrospective ablations isolate each change introduced by the selected program.
Grounded paper writing.
Language models already take on parts of the writing and reviewing process: AutoSurvey drafts literature surveys (Wang et al., 2024b), and GPT-4 feedback on manuscripts overlaps with human reviews about as much as two human reviewers overlap with each other (Liang et al., 2024).
Full-paper writers differ in how tightly they bind text to evidence.
PaperOrchestra writes a manuscript from whatever materials it is given, without being tied to a particular experiment pipeline (Song et al., 2026), whereas Spark-to-Paper, a set of skills inside a coding assistant, decides which evidence an experiment must produce before running it and checks the draft both deterministically and by self-critique (Qian et al., 2026).
Checks on the finished paper are needed too.
ReAgent compares an agent-written paper with its repository, reading the code for the described method and rerunning experiments for the reported numbers (Shen et al., 2026), and MLReplicate shows why: when experts reviewed the ten agent-generated manuscripts an automated review pipeline had accepted, 59% of their reviews flagged fabricated or unsupported claims (Gaddipati et al., 2026).
Our writing phase similarly maps each claim to its source artifacts, and runs an independent audit agent in every round of revision.
Scientific figure generation.
Beyond raster pipelines such as PaperBanana (Zhu et al., 2026a), a line of work treats a figure as a program that can be revised after generation.
DeTikZify synthesizes TikZ from sketches with a fine-tuned vision-language model (Belouadi et al., 2024); recent agentic systems instead start from a paper’s text.
AutoFigure iterates an SVG layout under a critic but renders the final figure with an image model from a text prompt derived from that layout (Zhu et al., 2026b), and AutoFigure-Edit and CraftEditor recover editability by tracing the rendered raster into SVG (Lin et al., 2026; Zhao et al., 2026).
LiveFigure has a VLM write python-pptx code with explicit coordinates and counts the manual edits needed to reach publication quality (Shao et al., 2026), while FigTree recursively decomposes a figure into SVG fragments refined by render-critic loops (Liu et al., 2026).
SciFig splits generation into planning, layout synthesis, component rendering, and VLM-guided refinement, and outputs XML diagrams that remain editable in standard diagramming tools (Huang et al., 2026a).
In all of these, geometry is delegated to the image model or emitted directly by the language model, and checked only after the fact, visually or with lightweight overlap heuristics, rather than enforced by construction.
autoillustrate instead has agents write coordinate-free draw.io templates and Python layout programs that must pass deterministic collision and routing checks, and confines the image model to a final restyle audited against the deterministic render.
Appendix B The Agentic Scientist: Additional Results
B.1 Details on Aggregated Sweep Task Discoveries
To illustrate the discoveries behind these aggregate scores, we highlight the manuscript with the highest AI reviewer score for each task, breaking ties by final task performance (Figure 19).
Figure 19: First pages of the five highlighted task manuscripts, selected by highest AI reviewer score and final task performance. The generated papers are watermarked by The Agentic Scientist.
Learned optimization.
Claude Code with Opus 5 broadens meta-training from clean dense-network tasks to a composite task that averages dense and convolutional network losses, with per-particle label corruption. It also adds horizon conditioning and probe-based checkpoint selection. Ablations attribute the development gains primarily to task diversity and label corruption, with little benefit from the latter additions. On sealed SVHN, performance remains near chance, and the small advantage over the reference mainly reflects one avoided divergence rather than better typical updates.
Sparse autoencoders.
Pi with Opus 5 replaces a one-shot TopK encoder with two residual-pursuit rounds followed by four learned sparse-code refinement steps, while preserving the sparsity budget. Intermediate reconstruction losses and changes to dictionary optimization accompany this richer encoder. Against matched TopK and BatchTopK references, held-out reconstruction error falls by 28.8–53.8%, but fitting takes approximately 40×40\times longer. A targeted feature-perturbation metric worsens, so the reconstruction gains do not establish improved interpretability.
Tabular distribution shift.
OpenCode with Opus 5 augments a TabICLv2 stage-one baseline trained from scratch with support-fitted rescaling and clipping, feature-permutation and class-rotation ensembling, and posterior correction using a class prior estimated from query predictions. Class-balanced training loss is also implemented, but ablations find no measurable benefit from that change. The deployment recipe raises sealed mean balanced accuracy from 0.606 to 0.778, while ordinary accuracy falls from 0.781 to 0.766, exposing a trade-off between macro-recall and aggregate accuracy.
World models.
OpenCode with DeepSeek Flash aligns TinyWorlds training and decoding with next-frame prediction: four clean context frames condition a fully masked fifth frame, and one prediction pass replaces iterative MaskGIT decoding. The selected model also enlarges the dynamics transformer and quantizer codebook. On sealed Pong, its mean relative rollout-error reduction against the reference is 49%. Ablations support the training and decoding changes, while removing the selected model’s constant-velocity latent initializer improves development results. Evidence remains limited to action-free Pong and one seed per evaluation setting.
Hyperparameter optimization.
Claude Code with Opus 5 replaces the incumbent-centred local-search baseline with a Gaussian-process optimizer using Expected Improvement. It selects a loss transformation per problem, averages over kernel hyperparameters for mixed search spaces, and refines acquisition candidates through trust-region proposals and coordinate search. Ablations identify target transformation as the largest measured contributor. Gains are strongest on mixed and hierarchical spaces; the comparisons match query counts rather than evaluation cost and do not establish superiority over differential evolution on continuous tasks.
