B.3 Error-Diffusion Additional Results
Bias offsets and Dale-style sign preservation
Because biases are unconstrained and may become negative, we ask whether bias offsets are large enough to functionally dominate the synaptic preactivation terms, which would weaken the interpretation of the learned activity as primarily shaped by Dale-style synaptic interactions.
We decomposed each preactivation as
z=zsyn+b,z=z_{\mathrm{syn}}+b,
and measured 𝔼​|b|/𝔼​|zsyn|\mathbb{E}|b|/\mathbb{E}|z_{\mathrm{syn}}|, the fraction of
elements for which |b|>|zsyn||b|>|z_{\mathrm{syn}}|, and the fraction for which
adding bb flips the preactivation sign. Across all 5000 validation examples,
bias effects were concentrated almost entirely in the first convolutional layer:
conv1_p
had a bias/synaptic ratio of 0.390, with biases dominating 30.5%30.5\% of preactivation elements and flipping 14.9%14.9\% of signs; conv1_n showed a similar ratio of 0.379, with 31.2%31.2\% bias-dominant elements and a 15.2%15.2\% sign-flip rate.
In deeper layers, bias effects were negligible: conv2
and conv3 had bias/synaptic ratios at most 0.0105 and sign-flip rates below
0.004, while all fully connected layers had ratios at most 0.0032 and sign-flip
rates below 0.001.
These results suggest that negative biases primarily act as threshold shifts, especially in the first convolutional layer, rather than as a mechanism for violating Dale-style synaptic signs.
The formal pathway-level excitatory and inhibitory structure remains intact, while bias offsets provide a limited stream-specific adjustment to activation thresholds.
