Appendix D Error Diffusion ALIFE Paper Details
D.1 Error Diffusion ALIFE Paper: Agent Draft to Submission Diff
As this paper was produced with a development version of The Agentic Scientist, its conceptual figure was originally generated with Nano Banana Pro (Google DeepMind, 2025), rather than with autoillustrate.
Since Nano Banana Pro outputs a raster image without an editable source, the human authors could not correct its factual inaccuracies in place: before submission, they kept the right panel, which was correct, and manually redrew the left and middle panels.
The current version of The Agentic Scientist uses autoillustrate by default for conceptual figures (Sections 3.5 and 3.6), which produces more faithful figures than the Nano Banana Pro-based PaperBanana pipeline (Zhu et al., 2026a) (Section 7).
For reference, we applied the current version of autoillustrate to the agent draft of the ALIFE paper, before any human edits and adjustments.
Figure 20 shows the resulting editable draw.io diagram and the enhanced figure derived from it.
(a) Editable draw.io diagram.
(b) Enhanced figure derived from the editable diagram.
Figure 20: autoillustrate applied to the agent draft of the Error Diffusion ALIFE paper, before human edits: the editable diagram (a) and its corresponding enhanced figure (b). The figure in the submitted and camera-ready versions of the ALIFE paper was instead generated with Nano Banana Pro as a non-editable raster image; the human authors kept its correct right panel and manually redrew the left and middle panels to correct their inaccuracies.
The prompt given to autoillustrate was: “Overview of the dual-stream Error Diffusion framework. Left: The excitatory/inhibitory architecture maintains separate streams (𝐩\mathbf{p}, 𝐧\mathbf{n}) with four non-negative weight matrices per layer, enforcing Dale’s principle structurally. Center: The Error Diffusion update broadcasts output error directly to hidden layers without weight transport or random feedback matrices. Right: The shared architecture is applied to classification (with domain-specific sigmoid widths, mean-centered error, and asymmetric initialization) and reinforcement learning (with ReLU activations and PPO integration).”
