Initial Task Instructions for Error-Diffusion Classification Topic Direction
## Goal
- Extend the binary classification Error Diffusion program to a multi-class classification problem. Achieve at least 95% `val_accuracy` on the fixed MNIST validation split, and aim for more than 98% `val_accuracy` if possible.
- Do not use the official MNIST test split during search-time evaluation and do not write `test_accuracy` into `_results.json`.
- Tip: Check gradient norm / activation norm at each epoch during training.
## Rubrics
- Do not use backprop or autograd for the backward pass.
- Do not use W.T (forward weights transposed) in the backward pass, because this is a sign of backprop.
- Make sure to use the MNIST data loader provided in `example_mnist_mlp.py`, using its fixed `55k train / 5k val / 10k test` split with `split_seed=0` and `500` validation examples per class.
- The training script must write `_results.json` with at least `val_accuracy`, `training_time`, `terminated_early`, `dataset_name`, `split_seed`, `train_examples`, `val_examples`, and `val_examples_per_class`.
- `dataset_name` must be exactly `"MNIST"`. Synthetic or toy datasets must not pass evaluation as MNIST.
- Downloaded datasets and caches must not be stored inside any `program_*` directory. Use a shared cache path such as `AGENTIC_DATA_DIR` when available instead of `./data` relative to the program workspace.
- Keep the core weight updating mechanism as delta w \propto -(learning rate) x (presynaptic activity) x (postsynaptic activation derivative) x (one global error sign)
- Polarity invariance must be preserved through multiple layers. For this, the following must hold:
- all trainable weight tensors should remain non-negative (this constraint may be relaxed if needed for performance). Any violations of the non-negative constraint should be logged (e.g., the number of violated weights).
- the derivative of the activation function must be non-negative.
- the forward pass must preserve the excitatory/inhibitory sign structure such as:
- `p_i = f(+p_{i-1} @ W_pp - n_{i-1} @ W_np + b_p)`
- `n_i = f(+n_{i-1} @ W_nn - p_{i-1} @ W_pn + b_n)`
- Do not use fixed random matrices or trainable backward weights to route output errors to hidden layers, because that would make the method equivalent to Direct Feedback Alignment / Feedback Alignment.
- Do not use any data augmentation.
- Do not rely on random feature extractors.
- Do not treat multi-class classification as a simple ensemble of binary ED models.
- Focus on fully connected layer variants; do not explore conv nets or attention.
- Achieve at least 95% validation accuracy on the fixed MNIST validation split.
Initial Task Instructions for Error-Diffusion Convolution Topic Direction
# Goal
- Extend the best multi-class Error Diffusion program from `ed_mnist` (provided as `program_0/src/program.py`) to a convolutional neural network. Achieve at least 60% `val_accuracy` (equivalent to `combined_score`) on the fixed CIFAR-10 validation split, and aim for more than 85% `val_accuracy` if possible.
- Do not use the official CIFAR-10 test split and do not write `test_accuracy` into `_results.json`.
- Tip: Check gradient norm / activation norm at each epoch during training.
# Rubrics
- Do not use backprop or autograd for the backward pass.
- Do not use W.T (forward weights transposed) in the backward pass, because
this is a sign of backprop.
- Make sure to use the same network structure as in `example_cifar10_convnet.py`, but implement it using Error Diffusion’s dual-stream architecture. The model must use convolutional layers. Do not collapse the task back into a flat MLP on handcrafted features.
- Make sure to use the CIFAR data loader provided in `example_cifar10_convnet.py`, using its fixed `45k train / 5k val / 10k test` split with `split_seed=0` and `500` validation examples per class.
- The training script must write `_results.json` with at least `val_accuracy`, `training_time`, `terminated_early`, `dataset_name`, `num_conv_layers`, `split_seed`, `train_examples`, `val_examples`, and `val_examples_per_class`.
- Print per-epoch training progress including the epoch number and validation accuracy in a parseable format such as `epoch 012 ... val_acc=0.2345` or `epoch 012 ... val=0.2345`. The evaluator may stop runs that are learning too slowly based on these logs.
- Optimize against validation accuracy.
- `dataset_name` must be exactly `"CIFAR10"`.
- Downloaded datasets and caches must not be stored inside any `program_*` directory. Use a shared cache path such as `AGENTIC_DATA_DIR` when available instead of `./data` relative to the program workspace.
- Polarity invariance must be preserved through multiple layers. For this, the
following must hold:
- all trainable weight tensors should remain non-negative (this can be relaxed if needed to achieve the desired performance).
- the derivative of the activation function must be non-negative.
- the forward pass must preserve the excitatory/inhibitory sign structure,
including convolutional layers, for example:
- `p_i = f(conv_op(p_{i-1}, W_pp) - conv_op(n_{i-1}, W_np) + b_p)`
- `n_i = f(conv_op(n_{i-1}, W_nn) - conv_op(p_{i-1}, W_pn) + b_n)`
- Do not use fixed random matrices or trainable backward weights to route output errors to hidden layers, because that would make the method equivalent to Direct Feedback Alignment / Feedback Alignment.
- Achieve at least 60% validation accuracy on the fixed CIFAR10 validation split.
